		{
			"id": "GlyZDNmq",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -1014.5979149508546,
			"y": 1447.3023412342325,
			"width": 2433.19970703125,
			"height": 350,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b0Q",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 213464011,
			"version": 707,
			"versionNonce": 816597733,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": null,
			"updated": 1784901709649,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "| Solution                | Basic idea                             | Main advantage                    | Main problem                                        |\n| ----------------------- | -------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| Database auto-increment | One database increments a counter      | Very simple                       | Central bottleneck and difficult with shards        |\n| Per-shard counters      | Each database has its own counter      | Scales better                     | Can produce duplicate IDs without a shard prefix    |\n|Rangeallocation+zookeeper| Give each server a block of IDs        | Few central calls                 | Range management and weak time ordering             |\n| Central ticket service  | One service returns the next ID        | Easy global uniqueness            | Network dependency and central bottleneck           |\n| Timestamp only          | Use current time                       | Simple and time ordered           | Many objects can be created in the same millisecond |\n| UUID v4                 | Generate a random 128-bit value        | No central service                | Larger than 64 bits and not naturally time ordered  |\n| Snowflake               | Combine time, machine and sequence     | Fast, compact and roughly ordered | Requires good clocks and unique worker IDs          |\n",
			"rawText": "| Solution                | Basic idea                             | Main advantage                    | Main problem                                        |\n| ----------------------- | -------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| Database auto-increment | One database increments a counter      | Very simple                       | Central bottleneck and difficult with shards        |\n| Per-shard counters      | Each database has its own counter      | Scales better                     | Can produce duplicate IDs without a shard prefix    |\n|Rangeallocation+zookeeper| Give each server a block of IDs        | Few central calls                 | Range management and weak time ordering             |\n| Central ticket service  | One service returns the next ID        | Easy global uniqueness            | Network dependency and central bottleneck           |\n| Timestamp only          | Use current time                       | Simple and time ordered           | Many objects can be created in the same millisecond |\n| UUID v4                 | Generate a random 128-bit value        | No central service                | Larger than 64 bits and not naturally time ordered  |\n| Snowflake               | Combine time, machine and sequence     | Fast, compact and roughly ordered | Requires good clocks and unique worker IDs          |\n",
			"fontSize": 28,
			"fontFamily": 8,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "middle",
			"containerId": "Mfg1WGBL",
			"originalText": "| Solution                | Basic idea                             | Main advantage                    | Main problem                                        |\n| ----------------------- | -------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| Database auto-increment | One database increments a counter      | Very simple                       | Central bottleneck and difficult with shards        |\n| Per-shard counters      | Each database has its own counter      | Scales better                     | Can produce duplicate IDs without a shard prefix    |\n|Rangeallocation+zookeeper| Give each server a block of IDs        | Few central calls                 | Range management and weak time ordering             |\n| Central ticket service  | One service returns the next ID        | Easy global uniqueness            | Network dependency and central bottleneck           |\n| Timestamp only          | Use current time                       | Simple and time ordered           | Many objects can be created in the same millisecond |\n| UUID v4                 | Generate a random 128-bit value        | No central service                | Larger than 64 bits and not naturally time ordered  |\n| Snowflake               | Combine time, machine and sequence     | Fast, compact and roughly ordered | Requires good clocks and unique worker IDs          |\n",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false
		},
