		{
			"id": "VtGNB8f3",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -2212.034644528554,
			"y": 22067.01878368573,
			"width": 6881.099609375,
			"height": 1370.5704381947965,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 4,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [
				"yy9yjmPfrGGBAXChwJZqE"
			],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b5q",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1674372389,
			"version": 765,
			"versionNonce": 1419603147,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": null,
			"updated": 1784901981644,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Company / Product    | Publicly Documented Approach         | Important Detail                                                                                                                 |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Discord              | Snowflake-style IDs                  | Discord documents a Twitter Snowflake-style format containing timestamp, worker, process, and increment fields.                  |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Twitter / X          | Snowflake                            | Twitter introduced Snowflake in 2010. The original public implementation was later retired and internally rewritten.             |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Shopify Payments     | ULID for some idempotency keys       | Shopify used sortable ULIDs instead of random UUIDv4 for one payment-related use case. This does not apply to every table.       |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Buildkite            | UUIDv7 for new tables                | Buildkite selected UUIDv7 as the primary key for new tables while preparing to shard its PostgreSQL database.                    |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| GitHub GraphQL API   | Opaque string IDs                    | GitHub tells clients to treat global IDs as opaque strings and not decode their internal structure.                              |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Google Cloud Spanner | UUIDv4 or distributed sequences      | Spanner recommends UUIDs for many applications and also supports bit-reversed integer sequences for INT64 or BIGINT needs.       |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| MongoDB              | ObjectId                             | MongoDB ObjectId is 12 bytes and contains a timestamp, random process value, and incrementing counter.                           |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+",
			"rawText": "+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Company / Product    | Publicly Documented Approach         | Important Detail                                                                                                                 |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Discord              | Snowflake-style IDs                  | Discord documents a Twitter Snowflake-style format containing timestamp, worker, process, and increment fields.                  |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Twitter / X          | Snowflake                            | Twitter introduced Snowflake in 2010. The original public implementation was later retired and internally rewritten.             |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Shopify Payments     | ULID for some idempotency keys       | Shopify used sortable ULIDs instead of random UUIDv4 for one payment-related use case. This does not apply to every table.       |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Buildkite            | UUIDv7 for new tables                | Buildkite selected UUIDv7 as the primary key for new tables while preparing to shard its PostgreSQL database.                    |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| GitHub GraphQL API   | Opaque string IDs                    | GitHub tells clients to treat global IDs as opaque strings and not decode their internal structure.                              |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Google Cloud Spanner | UUIDv4 or distributed sequences      | Spanner recommends UUIDs for many applications and also supports bit-reversed integer sequences for INT64 or BIGINT needs.       |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| MongoDB              | ObjectId                             | MongoDB ObjectId is 12 bytes and contains a timestamp, random process value, and incrementing counter.                           |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+",
			"fontSize": 64.49743238563748,
			"fontFamily": 8,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "middle",
			"containerId": "vTmHneFV",
			"originalText": "+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Company / Product    | Publicly Documented Approach         | Important Detail                                                                                                                 |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Discord              | Snowflake-style IDs                  | Discord documents a Twitter Snowflake-style format containing timestamp, worker, process, and increment fields.                  |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Twitter / X          | Snowflake                            | Twitter introduced Snowflake in 2010. The original public implementation was later retired and internally rewritten.             |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Shopify Payments     | ULID for some idempotency keys       | Shopify used sortable ULIDs instead of random UUIDv4 for one payment-related use case. This does not apply to every table.       |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Buildkite            | UUIDv7 for new tables                | Buildkite selected UUIDv7 as the primary key for new tables while preparing to shard its PostgreSQL database.                    |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| GitHub GraphQL API   | Opaque string IDs                    | GitHub tells clients to treat global IDs as opaque strings and not decode their internal structure.                              |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Google Cloud Spanner | UUIDv4 or distributed sequences      | Spanner recommends UUIDs for many applications and also supports bit-reversed integer sequences for INT64 or BIGINT needs.       |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| MongoDB              | ObjectId                             | MongoDB ObjectId is 12 bytes and contains a timestamp, random process value, and incrementing counter.                           |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false
		},
